<p>Shirley is the cleanest prewar case. 42366 girls in 1935, a name built by a child actress, and 4 years later it was at 20447. Hollywood handed the country a name, and the country used it up on schedule.</p>
<h2>Two names, one year</h2>
<p>In 1992, <a href="/name/Hillary/">Hillary</a> hit 2520 births. One year later it was 1064.</p>
<p><a href="/name/Chelsea/">Chelsea</a>, the same year, started from 16177 and took 2 years to reach half.</p>
<p>Two names, two speeds, one year. The record does not say what caused either collapse. It only shows that a name can be everywhere in a season and spent inside a couple of years.</p>
<p>A peak is invisible while it is happening. Parents in 1992 were choosing a name that was rising. The record, with hindsight, sees a name already at the top of its arc.</p>
