<h2>What this measures</h2>
<p>Every figure here counts births, not living people. A name that halves is not a name whose bearers are dying. It is a name that stopped being given.</p>
<p>The SSA record only includes names with at least five births in a year. A name that drops below five counts as zero here.</p>
<p>Every name gets the same 30 years. That means names that peaked after 1995 are not in this cohort — the record has not run long enough to judge them. Madison and Nevaeh are the obvious absences. Both may halve. Neither has had its window yet.</p>
<p>The half-life of a name''s living population is a different number, and it runs the other way: the Karens are still here, decades after their births collapsed. That measurement lives on the individual name pages — <a href="/name/Karen/">Karen</a>, <a href="/name/Chelsea/">Chelsea</a>, <a href="/name/Shirley/">Shirley</a>.</p>
<p>A name is a fashion with a death certificate. The certificate is public. This is the arithmetic of it.</p>', 'published', 'NobodyNamed', '/api/og/default', '2026-10-26T09:00:00.000Z', datetime('now'))
